    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change mirrors the codebase's established guard-step convention exactly, so a maintainer would find it entirely natural:\n\n- `EnsureDailyLimit` is a direct sibling of `EnsureCityCapacity` \u2014 same shape (private `static final` limit constant, `findAll().stream().filter().count()`, throws a checked escalation), and it declares only the parameters it needs (just `OwnerRepository`, correctly omitting `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` since the rule doesn't depend on the body). Logic sits in the right unit: a single-purpose function class, not bolted onto an existing one.\n- `DailyLimitExceededException` matches `CityAtCapacityException` (checked, message built in the constructor from the count).\n- `DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler` is identical in structure to `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`, reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build` helper rather than duplicating response-building logic, with the correct 429 mapping.\n- The escalation YAML and the POST pipeline wiring (`next: ensureDailyLimit`, `govern: [ transaction ]`) follow the documented pattern and slot cleanly into the existing `ensure*` chain.\n\nNothing is duplicated beyond the intended convention, and no class or method is becoming a catch-all. (The `findAll().stream().count()` approach has an efficiency cost, but that's a correctness/performance concern and matches the existing sibling pattern, so it's out of scope here and not a structural objection.)",
